00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.460
in the end is a different story, but, like, right away it was like, oh, this feels like

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.640
it's, I'm going on a quest and that's the kind of game I like.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:34.620
So, kind of along the same lines as Chubby Cherub, this kind of felt like what I was expecting

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:41.700
NES games to be like, you know, in that it just felt like a pretty kind of, not run-of-the-mill

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.220
video game because I feel like that's not really fair at this point.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:50.940
But from today's standards, kind of what you'd expect if you're, like, watching a movie about

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.520
a kid in the 80s playing some obscure video game, I feel like this one would fit, you know?

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:01.000
But yeah, I think, like, what Chum was saying, that it feels very solid in moving around and,

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.540
you know, it looks nice.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.820
Yeah. Overall, I was pretty pleased initially.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:11.580
Yeah, I mean, I was incredibly worried going into this game just because I see that Bandai

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.720
thing again and, you know, just a reminder that Bandai only holds the licenses for these

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.020
games. They're not making them.

